223 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /P
/P 129 0 R
/E (Block-based programming is a simple and visual method for creating digital projects without the need to know a complex programming language. Instead of writing code with text, students use colored blocks, which resemble puzzle pieces, to construct logical actions and sequences. Each block represents a command or action, such as "move a character," "play a sound," or "count to 10." )
/Pg 9 0 R
/K [9 10]
>>
endobj
224 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /P
/P 129 0 R
/E (By assembling them, a program is created that works smoothly and intuitively. This approach helps students understand the basics of programming by manipulating concepts in a concrete and fun way. Block-based programming is particularly suitable for beginners because it reduces errors and frustrations associated with writing code. It allows them to focus on logic and creativity, making technology accessible and fun, even for those who have never programmed before.)
/Pg 9 0 R
/K [11]
>>
endobj
